//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="577\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: type.int.shift
//!
//! wolf-lang#755 (s237, s755): a function with no `if`, loop, call or
//! index is lowered as one block, and its locals are kept for that
//! block alone. A construct that branches in the middle of an
//! expression ends the block, so a local declared before it and read
//! after it had no definition: a plain `<<` by a constant count of a
//! value that is not one (the round trip's check). Native and release
//! panicked here with s237's shift lowering before the #755 fix (s755)
//! landed, the shift's own branches being new, "use of a variable with
//! no definition on some path". The checked machine and lupin 0.1.52
//! printed what this row says.

fn f(x: u64) -> u64 {
    let y = x + 1
    let z = x << 3
    y + z
}

fn main() {
    print("{f(64)}")
}
